WEBVTT

00:00:00.040 --> 00:00:17.060
Could Earth microbes turn poisonous Martian dirt into soil like doing the entire job directly in the dirt with absolutely zero plumbing? That is what we are digging into today. It is called MarsWorm and specifically we are looking at their version three design today. Which

00:00:17.060 --> 00:00:29.520
is wild because previous versions of this terraforming idea relied on like massive hardware footprint you know tanks and wash basins and pipes. But version three tries to do it all natively.

00:00:29.620 --> 00:00:41.860
I mean doing it all natively sounds elegant ripping out the plumbing I get it but looking at these schematics it feels like it breaks the whole system. It is like um like removing the kitchen from a restaurant but still expecting your dinner to just arrive at the table.

00:00:42.060 --> 00:00:51.640
That is a great analogy because taking the tanks away does actually break three very specific things. First off without a dedicated tank to grow the food nothing feeds the other

00:00:51.640 --> 00:00:54.400
microbes. Oh so the whole biological supply chain vanishes.

00:00:54.780 --> 00:01:07.520
Exactly and the second thing is the Martian regolith itself. That crushed rock and dust is full of a toxic salt called perchlorate. When the microbes destroy that toxin it leaves chlorine behind.

00:01:07.860 --> 00:01:10.640
And in the old system the pipes would have washed that away right?

00:01:10.720 --> 00:01:14.140
Yeah but now that chlorine has nowhere to go it just sits there in the dirt.

00:01:14.260 --> 00:01:16.960
So you are basically swapping one poison for another.

00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:36.020
Right and then you have the third issue which is this massive biological clash because in the old setup tanks physically separated two crucial jobs destroying the perchlorate and fixing nitrogen. but without the tanks those two jobs have to happen in the exact same handful of dirt. Really just fight each other.

00:01:36.280 --> 00:01:39.460
Wait so how do you stop a microscopic turf war in a handful of dirt?

00:01:39.920 --> 00:01:50.340
Well it comes down to two major aha moments in the research. The first one fixes the food issue. It turns out an ordinary bacterium can build its body entirely from Mars air and hydrogen.

00:01:50.780 --> 00:01:53.640
Wait really just from the air and hydrogen?

00:01:53.860 --> 00:01:58.940
Yeah and completely in the dark too. Which means that previous lit tank design just isn't needed at

00:01:58.940 --> 00:02:07.320
all. Okay so the microbe acts like its own power plant that solves the food. But what about the turf war between the perchlorate cleaners and the nitrogen

00:02:07.320 --> 00:02:10.240
fixers? So that fight is actually an oxygen problem

00:02:10.450 --> 00:02:15.810
Oh I see so the cleaning crew's exhaust basically destroys the building crew's tools.

00:02:16.090 --> 00:02:23.570
That is exactly it. So the aha fix there is well you don't separate them in space you separate them in time you run the dirt bed in stages.

00:02:23.930 --> 00:02:26.070
Oh so you do them in turns so they just never meet.

00:02:26.310 --> 00:02:30.270
Right you clean the dirt first let the oxygen dissipate and then you fix the nitrogen.

00:02:30.470 --> 00:02:33.450
Okay but wait the chlorine still needs a way out right we still don't have pipes.

00:02:33.990 --> 00:02:39.550
Yeah and the project notes call this the honest price of losing the plumbing. The salt has to leave as a gas.

00:02:39.890 --> 00:02:41.630
How does a salt leave as a gas?

00:02:42.210 --> 00:02:49.430
Well one microbe gets exactly one borrowed gene from a salt marsh plant to literally blow the chlorine off as a gas.

00:02:49.770 --> 00:02:54.770
That is wild just one genetic tweak replaces an entire mechanical washing system.

00:02:55.390 --> 00:03:03.950
Oh yeah the cheapest most fatal experiment that could just end the whole project. Nobody has ever actually grown these specific bacteria in Martian regolith.

00:03:04.390 --> 00:03:11.970
Right and when scientists tried growing other microbes in a Mars simulant they kept only a tenth of their growth just a tenth.

00:03:12.090 --> 00:03:25.530
Yeah it is a huge bottleneck and by the way if you are listening and want all the heavy details and the exact protocols for that jar on a shelf experiment they all live on the MarsWorm website. We really recommend you go there to explore the hard numbers for yourself.

00:03:25.710 --> 00:03:42.670
Definitely go check that out because it brings us right back to that restaurant without a kitchen. I mean if these microbes can't survive a simple jar of dirt on a shelf none of the fancy engineering matters. Which really makes you wonder what other grand space ambitions are just one cheap shelf experiment away from being grounded?
